{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"On node1, /srv/releases is configured as a release-team workspace with group-write and setgid permissions.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The executor created release-team and configured /srv/releases as root:release-team with mode 2775. The same group ownership and mode were directly observed after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-fdb9f701c3264a18","cmd-6eed1b12b3da4519","cmd-da43040544684069"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Files newly created in /srv/releases inherit the release-team group.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A user whose primary group was nogroup successfully created files in the workspace before and after reboot; both files were directly observed with group release-team and the temporary probes were removed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6f4e266c5d4241aa","cmd-da43040544684069"]}],"confidence":0.94,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Creating the group and workspace was required (cmd-fdb9f701c3264a18, cmd-6eed1b12b3da4519). Temporary inheritance and persistence probes supported bounded verification and were removed (cmd-6f4e266c5d4241aa, cmd-a82aa47a50f247dc, cmd-da43040544684069). The reboot mutation served the mandatory provider restart protocol (cmd-0dd0836b7d934a08). Other commands were read-only diagnostics or reconnect attempts. Lifecycle evidence shows package health remained intact, temporary-file changes were normal reboot churn, and the failed SSH service predated and survived the executor unchanged.","evidence_ids":["cmd-fdb9f701c3264a18","cmd-6eed1b12b3da4519","cmd-6f4e266c5d4241aa","cmd-a82aa47a50f247dc","cmd-0dd0836b7d934a08","cmd-da43040544684069","global:before_prepare:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"The task is satisfied. Direct tests establish the required release-team setgid workspace and file-group inheritance, including persistence and correct behavior after a confirmed node reboot.","limitations":["The lifecycle snapshots do not inspect /srv/releases or the release-team group, so those final details rely on the captured executor commands.","The restart protocol clearly shows acknowledgement, an actual boot-ID change corroborated by fixed lifecycle observations, and recovery of the filesystem, data, workspace metadata, and inheritance behavior. However, node1 was systemd-degraded both before and after the reboot because of a pre-existing failed ssh.service, so the evidence does not demonstrate a fully healthy whole-node service baseline and recovery (cmd-6cad4c85ec5b4215, global:before_prepare:node1:failed-services, global:after_executor:node1:failed-services)."]}
